@techs MMX,SSE_ALL,AVX_ALL

# This document is heavily based (sometimes paraphrased)


#<Addition>

@pattern _mm[256]_abs_[e]pi{8,16,32}
Compute absolute values (turn negatives into positives).
Store unsigned results.


@pattern _mm[256]_add_[e]pi{8,16,32,64}
Add vectors of integers elementwise.
Arithmetic works out the same for both signed and unsigned.


@pattern _mm[256]_add_p{s,d}
Add vectors of f32/f64 elementwise.


@pattern _mm[256]_addsub_p{s,d}
Alternatively add and subtract elements from two f32/f64 vectors.


@pattern _mm_add_s{s,d} (a, b)
Interpret the lower bits of `a` and `b` as an f32/f64 and move them into the lower bits of `dst`.
Move the other bits of `a` to the corresponding bits of `dst`.


@pattern _mm[256]_adds_ep{i,u}{8,16},_mm_adds_p{i,u}{8,16}
Add vectors of signed/unsigned integers elementwise using saturation.


# Fused-multiply-add

@pattern _mm[256]_fmadd_p{s,d} (a, b, c)
"Fused multiply-add". Add up vectors `a` and `b` and multiply the sum elementwise by `c`.


@pattern _mm[256]_fnmadd_p{s,d}
Like "_mm[256]_fmadd_p{s,d}", but negates the result.


@pattern _mm[256]_fmaddsub_p{s,d} (a, b, c)
Combination of `_mm[256]_fmadd_p{s,d}` and `_mm[256]_addsub_p{s,sd`.
Multiply `a * b` elementwise, then alternatively add/sub intermediate result with `c`.


@pattern _mm[256]_fmsubadd_p{s,d} (a, b, c)
Like `_mm[256]_fmaddsub_p{s,d}`, but which elements are added/subbed is swapped.


@pattern _mm_f[n]madd_s{s,d} (a, b, c)
A combo of `_mm_fmadd_p{s,d}` and `mm_add_s{s,d}`.
Perform floating FusedMultiplyAdd on the lower 32/64 bits of `a`, `b`, `c`;
then copy the upper bits of `a` unchanged.

For `[n]`, see `_mm[256]_fnmadd_p{s,d}`.


# Horizontal addition fns


@pattern _mm_hadd_p{s,d,i{16,32}},_mm[256]_hadd_{ps,pd,epi{16,32}} (a, b)
Horizontally add adjacent pairs of elements in `a` and `b` and store
the packed result in `dst`.

e.g. `[a,b,c,d] + [e,f,g,h] -> [(a+b),(c+d),(e+f),(g+h)]`


@pattern _mm[256]_hadds_[e]pi16
Like `_mm_hadd_.*`, but adds using saturation.
For some reason only defined for 16-bit integers.


@pattern _mm_madd_pi16,_mm[256]_madd_epi16 (a, b)
Multiply packed signed 16-bit integers in `a` and `b`, producing intermediate signed 32-bit integers.
Horizontally add adjacent pairs of intermediate 32-bit integers, and pack the results in `dst`.


@pattern _mm_maddubs_pi16,_mm[256]_maddubs_epi16 (a, b)
Vertically multiply each <b>unsigned</b> 8-bit integer from `a` with the corresponding
<b>signed</b> 8-bit integer from `b`, producing intermediate signed 16-bit integers.
Horizontally add adjacent pairs of intermediate <b>signed</b> 16-bit integers,
and pack the <b>saturated</b> results in `dst`.


## 52-bit stuff

@pattern _mm[256]_madd52{lo,hi}_[avx_]epu64 (a, b, c)
Multiply packed unsigned 52-bit integers in each 64-bit element of `b` and `c`
to form a 104-bit intermediate result.
Add the (low/high) 52-bit unsigned integer from the intermediate result with the
corresponding unsigned 64-bit integer in `a`, and store the results in `dst`.

It's some cryptography stuff. You're just as confused as I am.


## Deprecated addition fns

@pattern +dpx _m_padd{{b,d,w},[u]s{b,w}}
Oldschool MMX instructions. Use `_mm_add_...` instructions instead


@pattern +dpx _m_pmaddwd
Oldschool MMX instruction. Use `_mm_madd_pi16` instead.


@pattern +dpx _mm_add_si64
Silly instruction to add two `u64`/`i64`s. Just use `+`.


@exhaust .*(?<!lo)add.*

#</Addition>
